  "plot_summary": "Reed discovers something inside a meteorite that landed on a farmer's land and indicates that \"this <i>proves</i> that some form of life <i>must</i> exist in outer space!\"&nbsp; (<i>Geez</i>, you'd think that their encounter with the Skrulls in issue 2, not to <i>mention</i> the Super-Skrull from a couple of issues ago, the trip they took out to Planet X in #7, the story that Dr. Doom told them about where he was in #10, their encounter with the Impossible Man in #11, and the ruins they found on the Moon in #13 - you'd think that would be <i>more</i> than enough evidence!)\n\nBut before Reed can study it any more, Johnny sees a \"huge globe\" floating over the crowds down in the street below.&nbsp; When they investigate, they all get sucked into it to find themselves face-to-face with the Watcher (who initially appeared in [LINK:1963:FF_13|FF #13]).&nbsp; His race is prevented from interfering, but a menace to the entire universe has appeared, and he knows the FF could possibly help.&nbsp; He shows them the accident at ACME Atomics Corp that turned a hapless worker into the Molecule Man!\n\nWhen they return, the Baxter Building is gone!&nbsp; But they soon see it hovering in the air above Times Square!&nbsp; The Molecule Man now declares he's in total control of the world!&nbsp; He's brandishing a \"molecular wand\" (<i>we've been given no idea where this wand came from</i>).&nbsp; A couple of hazards follow, but then he creates two magnets which adhere to Mr. Fantastic's flexible body.&nbsp; He points out that one is \"positive\" and one is \"negative\" which repel one another, stretching Mr. Fantastic's body as they fly in opposite directions, but <i>what?</i>  (I'm sure some reader somewhere pointed out that opposite magnets actually <i>attract</i> rather than <i>repel</i>, but we'll overlook that for now.)&nbsp; Johnny melts the magnets.\n\nSeveral pages of attempting to tackle the Molecule Man (including invisible Sue trying to wrest his wand away from him, to no avail) ultimately drive them to flee the scene to think of a plan to defeat him.&nbsp; Naturally the watching crowd brand them \"quitters.\"&nbsp; The Molecule Man orders the FF be found, so now they're being hunted by everyone!&nbsp; But they're rescued by one of the Yancy Street Gang and secretly hide in Alicia's apartment.\n\nReed then realizes that the MM can only affect <i>inorganic</i> molecules - nothing that's actually <i>alive</i>, and this gives him an idea.&nbsp; Alicia coats them with some plaster and then summons MM, who attempts to rearrange the molecules of what he thinks are statues, but the attempt backfires, and the feedback is \"too painful for him to endure!\"&nbsp; After falling, Reed grabs his wand, and at that moment, the huge glowing sphere of the Watcher appears and absorbs the Molecule Man.&nbsp; The Watcher was seeing everything, and stepped in to take MM after they had defeated him.&nbsp; He restores the Baxter Building to its proper location and will \"take the Molecule Man away...he will never menace you again!\"&nbsp; (<i>Fat chance!</i>)\n\nThe world is now safe again, and Reed wonders what to do with \"this now-harmless little wand!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "A better-than-reader grade issue but still below mid-grade.&nbsp; The Molecule Man is a significant threat, as we see numerous times over the decades ahead, but this was the first time I'd actually sat down to read his initial appearance.&nbsp; (Side note - my first exposure to him was in FF #187 - still with a wand, but a <i>much</i> cooler looking one!)\n\nThe book gets points off for never mentioning what the deal with the wand is.&nbsp; The accident affects the hapless worker, as the Watcher's flashback tale portrays, and his face is transformed, leaving us with the impression that he <i>also</i> has been transformed to have this mind-boggling power over molecules, <i>but the wand seems to come out of nowhere</i> - nothing is said of its origin, so it's a complete mystery.&nbsp; An accident that transforms a man and somehow mystically forms a wand, without which he's powerless?&nbsp; This seems hard to believe, particularly since Jack Kirby did such a <i>fantastic job</i> with MM's appearance.&nbsp; Not only do we see the lightning bolt lines emanating out of the middle of his face, the jagged mouth, making him look even <i>more</i> sinister, was so well done, I awarded an extra half point for that alone!&nbsp; What a <i>great</i> looking villain!&nbsp; <i>Superb job there, Jack!</i>\n\nBut I found the plot mildly interesting.&nbsp; Obviously, with a power like the Molecule Man, it must have been somewhat fun for Kirby to have dreamed up one threat after another for the FF to battle, conjured up out of nearby molecules and letting Stan fill in the dialog - this seems the perfect type of issue for that sort of working arrangement, kind of like what the X-Men's Danger Room is, where Kirby's imagination is free to run wild, as long as they leave enough pages to wrap things up!\n\nThe Watcher showing up to alert the FF of the threat of the Molecule Man seems to almost be unnecessary since while they were gone, MM snatches the Baxter Building anyway, so the Watcher <i>kind</i> of interferes by removing them from the scene as it's being taken away to fill them in on who they're up against.<i>  </i>But it's never really been possible to understand the Watcher's motives in most of his appearances.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n\nQuestion:&nbsp; Why does Johnny stand around in FF headquarters always flamed on?&nbsp; Wouldn't he rather conserve his limited flame to when he needs it?&nbsp; Yeah yeah, okay, it's more visually appealing this way, especially for the splash page - I get it, but still....&nbsp; At least he has the sense to flame off when they're at Alicia's apartment!",
  "letters_page": "None other than future Marvel writer/editor Mark Gruenwald (then only 10 years old) writes in that his FF fan club (which has him as secretary) loves the pin-up pages \"because they're perfect for decorating our clubhouse.\"&nbsp; <i>What</i>?&nbsp; <i>Oh Mark - you <b>didn't</b> cut up all those wonderful old (not to mention <b>valuable</b>) FF issues, did you?&nbsp; Argh!</i>\n\nHeh, Don Keefer writes:&nbsp; \"Dear Stan and Jack, How come we are supposed to write to 'Dear Stan and Jack' instead of 'Dear Jack and Stan'???\"&nbsp; with the response being:&nbsp; <i>Because it's Stan's typewriter and so HE makes up the rules - you troublemaker!</i>\n\nWhen Kevin Shields says \"...I only bought #13 for the sake of something to read.\" the editor replies with:&nbsp; <i>You only bought it for something to READ?&nbsp; What do you think the REST of us buy them for - paper dolls?</i>  (<i>Heh</i> - well done!)\n\nThe letters page also includes one from George R. Martin - the creator of the <i>A Song of Ice and Fire</i> series that led to <i>Game of Thrones</i>.",
  "ownership": "As for me, I got two just under mid-grade copies as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid $27.50 and $36.85 for.&nbsp; They are prepped and listed on eBay for sale, so we'll see what happens....",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}